.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt EVP_SIGNATURE-ML-DSA 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm EVP_SIGNATURE-ML-DSA ,
.Nm EVP_SIGNATURE-ML-DSA-44 ,
.Nm EVP_SIGNATURE-ML-DSA-65 ,
.Nm EVP_SIGNATURE-ML-DSA-87 ,
.Nd EVP_SIGNATURE ML-DSA support
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Sy ML-DSA-44 ,
.Sy ML-DSA-65
and
.Sy ML-DSA-87
EVP_PKEY implementations support key generation, and one-shot sign and
verify using the ML-DSA signature schemes described in
.Lk https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/204/final .
.Pp
The different algorithms names correspond to the parameter sets defined
in
.Lk https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/204/final
Section 4 Table 1. (The signatures range in size from ~2.5K to ~4.5K
depending on the type chosen).
There are 3 different security categories also depending on the type.
.Pp
.Xr EVP_SIGNATURE_fetch 3
can be used to explicitely fetch one of the 3 algorithms which can then
be used with
.Xr EVP_PKEY_sign_message_init 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_sign 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_message_init 3 ,
and
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify 3
to perform one-shot message signing or signature verification.
.Pp
The normal signing process (called Pure ML-DSA Signature Generation)
encodes the message internally as 0x00 || len(ctx) || ctx || message.
where
.Sy ctx
is some optional value of size 0x00..0xFF. This process is defined in
.Lk https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/204/final
Algorithm 2 step 10 and Algorithm 3 step 5. OpenSSL also allows the
message to not be encoded which is required for testing.
OpenSSL does not support Pre Hash ML-DSA Signature Generation, but this
may be done by the user by doing Pre hash encoding externally and then
choosing the option to not encode the message.
.Ss ML-DSA Signature Parameters
The following parameter can be used for both signing and verification.
it may be set by passing an OSSL_PARAM array to
.Xr EVP_PKEY_sign_message_init 3
or
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_message_init 3
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqcontext-string" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_CONTEXT_STRING Ns ) <octet string>
A string of octets with length at most 255. By default it is the empty
string.
.El
.Pp
The following parameters can be used when signing: They can be set by
passing an OSSL_PARAM array to
.Xr EVP_PKEY_sign_init_ex2 3 .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqmessage-encoding" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_MESSAGE_ENCODING Ns ) <integer>
The default value of 1 uses 'Pure ML-DSA Signature Generation' as
described above.
Setting it to 0 does not encode the message, which is used for testing.
The message encoding steps are defined in
.Lk https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/204/final
Algorithm 2 step 10 and Algorithm 3 step 5.
.It \(dqtest-entropy" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_TEST_ENTROPY Ns ) <octet string>
Used for testing to pass an optional deterministic per message random
value.
If set the size must be 32 bytes.
.It \(dqdeterministic" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_DETERMINISTIC Ns ) <integer>
The default value of 0 causes the per message randomness to be randomly
generated using a DRBG. Setting this to 1 causes the per message
randomness to be set to 32 bytes of zeros.
This value is ignored if "test-entropy" is set.
.It \(dqmu" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_MU Ns ) <integer>
The default value of 0 causes sign and verify operations to process a
raw message.
Setting this to 1 causes those operations to assume the input is the
.Qo Li mu Qc
value from
.Lk https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/204/final
Algorithm 7 step 6 and Algorithm 8 step 7.
.Pp
Note that the message encoding steps from
.Lk https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/204/final
Algorithm 2 step 10 and Algorithm 3 step 5 are omitted when this setting
is 1.
.El
.Pp
See
.Xr EVP_PKEY-ML-DSA 7
for information related to
.Sy ML-DSA
keys.
.Sh NOTES
For backwards compatability reasons
.Xr EVP_DigestSignInit_ex 3 ,
.Xr EVP_DigestSign 3 ,
.Xr EVP_DigestVerifyInit_ex 3
and
.Xr EVP_DigestVerify 3
may also be used, but the digest passed in
.Em mdname
must be NULL.
.Sh EXAMPLES
To sign a message using an ML-DSA EVP_PKEY structure:
.Bd -literal
    void do_sign(EVP_PKEY *key, unsigned char *msg, size_t msg_len)
    {
        size_t sig_len;
        unsigned char *sig = NULL;
        const OSSL_PARAM params[] = {
            OSSL_PARAM_octet_string("context-string", (unsigned char *)"A context string", 16),
            OSSL_PARAM_END
        };
        EVP_PKEY_CTX *sctx = EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_pkey(NULL, pkey, NULL);
        EVP_SIGNATURE *sig_alg = EVP_SIGNATURE_fetch(NULL, "ML-DSA-65", NULL);

        EVP_PKEY_sign_message_init(sctx, sig_alg, params);
        /* Calculate the required size for the signature by passing a NULL buffer. */
        EVP_PKEY_sign(sctx, NULL, &sig_len, msg, msg_len);
        sig = OPENSSL_zalloc(sig_len);
        EVP_PKEY_sign(sctx, sig, &sig_len, msg, msg_len);
        ...
        OPENSSL_free(sig);
        EVP_SIGNATURE_free(sig_alg);
        EVP_PKEY_CTX_free(sctx);
    }
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
